=2n: sum = (2n+1)/(2n+2)·(1+1) = (2n+1)/(2n+2)·2 = (2n+1)/(n+1). ✓✓\n\nGreat, the identity is proven.\n\nSo the full derivation is solid. Let me also double-check the relation U = (A + (-1)^n/C(2n,n))/2.\n\nU = sum_{j=n}^{2n} (-1)^j/C(2n,j).\nA = sum_{j=0}^{2n} (-1)^j/C(2n,j) = sum_{j=0}^{n-1} + (-1)^n/C(2n,n) + sum_{j=n+1}^{2n}.\nBy symmetry sum_{j=0}^{n-1} = sum_{j=n+1}^{2n} (substituting j→2n-j: (-1)^{2n-j}/C(2n,2n-j) = (-1)^{-j}/C(2n,j) = (-1)^j/C(2n,j) since (-1)^{2n}=1). \n\nSo A = 2·sum_{j=0}^{n-1} + (-1)^n/C(2n,n).\nU = (-1)^n/C(2n,n) + sum_{j=n+1}^{2n} = (-1)^n/C(2n,n) + sum_{j=0}^{n-1}.\nsum_{j=0}^{n-1} = (A - (-1)^n/C(2n,n))/2.\nU = (-1)^n/C(2n,n) + (A - (-1)^n/C(2n,n))/2 = (2(-1)^n/C(2n,n) + A - (-1)^n/C(2n,n))/2 = (A + (-1)^n/C(2n,n))/2. ✓\n\nT(n) = (2n)! (-1)^n U = (2n)!(-1)^n (A + (-1)^n/C(2n,n))/2\n= (2n)!(-1)^n A/2 + (2n)!(-1)^n·(-1)^n/(2 C(2n,n))\n= (2n)!(-1)^n A/2 + (2n)!/(2 C(2n,n))\n= (2n)!(-1)^n A/2 + (n!)^2/2.\n\nA = (2n+1)/(n+1). So first term = (2n)!(-1)^n (2n+1)/(2(n+1)).\n\nT(n) = (2n)!(-1)^n(2n+1)/(2(n+1)) + (n!)^2/2.\nS(n) = T(n) - (n!)^2 = (2n)!(-1)^n(2n+1)/(2(n+1)) - (n!)^2/2. ✓\n\nNow final numeric answer for n=10:\nS(10) = 20!·21/22 - (10!)^2/2 = 2322316553259520000 - 6584094720000 = 2322309969164800000.\n\nLet me re-verify the subtraction once more carefully.\n2322316553259520000\n-   6584094720000\n\n6584094720000 = 6,584,094,720,000.\n2,322,316,553,259,520,000 - 6,584,094,720,000 = 2,322,309,969,164,800,000.\n\nLet me verify: 2,322,309,969,164,800,000 + 6,584,094,720,000 = 2,322,316,553,259,520,000.\n2,322,309,969,164,800,000\n+       6,584,094,720,000\n= 2,322,316,553,259,520,000. \n\nCheck: 9,969,164,800,000 + 6,584,094,720,000 = 16,553,259,520,000. ✓ (9,969,164,800,000 + 6,584,094,720,000: 9996+6584=16580... let me add: 9,969,164,800,000 + 6,584,094,720,000 = 16,553,259,520,000. 9,969,164,800,000+6,000,000,000,000=15,969,164,800,000; +584,094,720,000=16,553,259,520,000. ✓)\n\nSo S(10) = 2,322,309,969,164,800,000 = 2322309969164